# Routh II.Arts.140-142 — the two quadrics whose intersection IS the polhode
# — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "The Advanced Part of a Treatise on the Dynamics of a System of
# Rigid Bodies", Part II (Macmillan, 6th ed. 1905), Art. 140 gives the two
# fundamental equations of a body turning under no forces,
#
#     A^2 w1^2 + B^2 w2^2 + C^2 w3^2 = G^2        (angular momentum)
#     A   w1^2 + B   w2^2 + C   w3^2 = T          (vis viva)
#
# and Art. 142 confirms the second is constant because no forces act. Two
# quadric surfaces in the space of angular velocities; the motion is confined
# to BOTH, so the instantaneous axis runs along their curve of intersection --
# the polhode of Art. 149. Everything Poinsot draws is a picture of these two
# equations.
#
# WHAT THIS NOTEBOOK SHOWS. That the two quadrics really are invariant while
# the thing they constrain is not. A tumbling body is set going and three
# numbers are printed repeatedly:
#
#     G = |L|              must not move
#     E = T/2              must not move   (posim's energy is half Routh's T)
#     |w|                  moves a great deal
#
# It is easy to assume the angular velocity is "the" conserved thing in a
# torque-free motion. It is not; neither its direction nor its magnitude is
# conserved. Only the two quadrics are, and the wandering of w inside them is
# the entire subject of Chapter IV.
#
# THE NUMBERS. A cuboid of mass 3 with half-extents (0.5, 1, 2) gives
# I = diag(5, 4.25, 1.25). Started with w = (0.3, 3, 0.1),
#
#     L   = I w = (1.5, 12.75, 0.125)
#     G   = |L| = 12.838540610209558
#     T   = A w1^2 + B w2^2 + C w3^2 = 38.7125     (Routh's T)
#     E   = T/2 = 19.35625                         (what posim's ENERGY prints)
#     |w| at t = 0 = 3.0166206257996713            (this one will change)
#
# The disturbance is deliberately asymmetric so the motion sits clearly off the
# separatrix G^2 = B*T of Art. 184 -- here G^2 - B*T = +0.3 -- and the body
# genuinely tumbles rather than creeping along the separatrix.
#
# SETUP NOTE. system.g_constant = 0. Only one body is present so gravity would
# do nothing anyway, but leaving G = 1 in a multi-body version of this test is
# how a purely rotational result gets quietly ruined by a translational
# singularity.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: a box tumbling irregularly end over end. The hud's E and L
# do not move at all while it does.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/youruser/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p2_two_quadrics
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_two_quadrics.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_two_quadrics.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15

new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], angular_velocity = [0.3, 3, 0.1] }

get obj0.inertia_tensor
get obj0.angular_momentum
norm(obj0.angular_momentum)
energy
norm(obj0.angular_velocity)

run 3 steps 3
norm(obj0.angular_momentum)
energy
norm(obj0.angular_velocity)

run 3 steps 3
norm(obj0.angular_momentum)
energy
norm(obj0.angular_velocity)

run 3 steps 3
norm(obj0.angular_momentum)
energy
norm(obj0.angular_velocity)
reset

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], angular_velocity = [0.3, 3, 0.1] }

scene create
scene set_time_step 0.03
